         <title><![CDATA[Lions and tigers are kept legally in Ontario backyards. No one knows how many there are]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/ontario-exotic-animal-ownership-9.7393011?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/a0f6723a-3c0d-44dc-8d80-ac28091ed219,1792762177178/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%289%2C322%2C2183%2C1227%29%3B' alt='Two tigers stand behind a fence' width='620' height='349' title='Two privately-owned tigers, seen here in an enclosure in Wainfleet, Ont. There are no federal or provincial rules when it comes to the ownership of dangerous exotic animals in Ontario, leaving monitoring and enforcement up to municipalities.'/><p>Lions and tigers are among some of the most recognizable and dangerous predators in the world. They're also legal to own in Ontario, where provincial authorities can't say how many are being held in private cages and backyards.</p>]]></description>
